# One home per rule

Every instruction, convention, or fact has **exactly one home**. If it needs to be mentioned elsewhere, **point** at the home. Do not copy the policy. Two copies will drift and conflict.

When adding or changing a rule: edit the home only. Update pointers if the path changed. Do not paste the new wording into a second file.

## Homes

| Topic | Home |
| --- | --- |
| What the package is (quantities, non-goals, FRs, NFRs, milestones) | `docs/design/requirements.md` |
| Language and distribution/import names | NFR-1 and NFR-2 in that file |
| How to write FRs | `.cursor/rules/functional-requirements.mdc` |
| Agent voice (not “educational” / “student”) | `.cursor/rules/project-purpose.mdc` |
| Layers, numerics, RNG *threading*, L3 vs L4 imports | `docs/design/layers.md` |
| Branches, commits, PRs, M0 toolchain | `.cursor/engineering-conventions.md` |
| PR form GitHub loads | `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md` |
| This rule | this file |

## Exceptions (do not treat as a second home)

1. **Cursor `.mdc` loaders** — a short rule may exist only so the agent opens the home. It **must** say “read \<path\>” and **must not** restate the policy (no copied bullet lists).
2. **Tool-required paths** — e.g. the GitHub PR template must live under `.github/`. That file *is* the home; conventions only link to it.
3. **Index lines** — a one-line “X lives in Y” in a companion-docs or out-of-scope list is a pointer, not a copy.

NFR-7 (must use `Generator`) lives in `requirements.md`. How L5 passes that generator into L2 lives in `layers.md`. Those are different facts; do not merge them into two full copies of NFR-7.
